  "deliberate_lookahead": {
    "what": "this producer CONTAINS the defect it measures, on purpose, and says so here rather than leaving a reader to find it. In the as_committed arm of every site, the admitted set is built from a reduction over later-half rows and then a threshold is taken over that set: at the ws3 site the expression is np.quantile(mean_e[elig], TOXIC_Q) with elig carrying the later count. That is the same shape checks/chk_decision_lookahead.py names in the five producers, and it is here because reproducing the rule is the only way to price it",
    "why_it_is_not_the_same_fault": "the five producers take a DECISION under that population -- a decile cut whose members are then reported as a finding about counterparties. This node takes no decision under it: the population is the object of measurement, and the two arms beside it exist precisely to say what the rule costs. A static reading cannot tell an auditor of a rule from a user of it, so the distinction is declared here in words and the gate is left to fire",
    "how_to_read_a_finding_against_this_file": "a lookahead finding naming survivorship_census.py is expected and is not a regression. What would be a regression is the early_only arm acquiring a later-half term, because that arm's whole claim is that it consults nothing after the ranking; the axis gate above compares the three arms' later-window floors on every run and stops the run if that arm ever demands more than zero"
  },
  "what_this_licenses": "a statement that the five producers rank counterparties whose return to the later window is already known, together with the size of that selection and the share of it the threshold rather than the data imposes. It does NOT license a claim that any published number is wrong: the committed arm reproduces every published anchor it was calibrated on, and the counterfactuals are what those numbers become under a different admission rule, not corrections to them"
